{"answer":"There are 7 subsets. One example is {1,8}. Exhaustively: a one-element subset cannot sum to 9 because 9 is not in the set; four or more distinct positive elements have sum at least 1+2+3+4=10. The two-element subsets are {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {4,5}; the three-element subsets are {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, {2,3,4}. Thus the total is 4+3=7.","approaches":[{"description":"Classify a subset summing to 9 by its cardinality, rule out sizes 1 and at least 4, and enumerate sizes 2 and 3.","id":"cardinality-enumeration","outcome":"candidate","reason":"The cardinality bounds leave only two finite cases, each enumerated without duplication."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived in steps size-bounds through total-count.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"count-seven","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly 7 subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} have sum 9.","step_ids":["size-bounds","pairs","triples","total-count"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Listed in step pairs.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"example-subset","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"{1,8} is a subset with sum 9.","step_ids":["pairs"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"size-bounds","justification":"A singleton would have to be {9}, which is unavailable. A subset of at least four distinct positive integers has sum at least 1+2+3+4=10. Thus only cardinalities 2 and 3 remain.","statement":"Any subset under consideration has either two or three elements."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["size-bounds"],"id":"pairs","justification":"For a pair {a,b} with a<b and a+b=9, take a=1,2,3,4; then b=8,7,6,5 respectively. Taking a>=5 would force b<=4, contrary to a<b.","statement":"The two-element subsets are {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, and {4,5}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["size-bounds"],"id":"triples","justification":"Write a<b<c. If a=1, then b+c=8 and b is 2 or 3, giving {1,2,6} and {1,3,5}. If a=2, then b+c=7 and b=3, giving {2,3,4}. If a>=3, then a+b+c>=3+4+5=12.","statement":"The three-element subsets are {1,2,6}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["pairs","triples"],"id":"total-count","justification":"The two lists have 4 and 3 members, respectively; their different cardinalities make them disjoint. Step size-bounds excludes every other cardinality.","statement":"The exhaustive total is 4+3=7."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
